\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Released-checkpoint weight perturbation (experiment A1). (a) PURE--REC gap vs.\ Gaussian weight-noise scale $\sigma $ (two seeds per $\sigma >0$; baseline at $\sigma =0$). The reversal is preserved within $\pm 0.7$ of $+10.24$ for $\sigma \leq 3\!\times \!10^{-3}$, where dev BLEU-4 stays above $12.7$ (above every reconstruction's maximum of $10.9$). (b) Dev BLEU-4, REC BLEU-4, PURE BLEU-4, and (at baseline) train-pool readout BLEU-4. The reversal and competence collapse together at $\sigma \geq 10^{-2}$; $\sigma =10^{-1}$ produces an all-empty-decoding model and is omitted. The reversal is locally robust at competence parity and tracks competence, not identity.\relax }}{18}{figure.caption.45}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:released_perturbation}{{4}{18}{Released-checkpoint weight perturbation (experiment A1). (a) PURE--REC gap vs.\ Gaussian weight-noise scale $\sigma $ (two seeds per $\sigma >0$; baseline at $\sigma =0$). The reversal is preserved within $\pm 0.7$ of $+10.24$ for $\sigma \leq 3\!\times \!10^{-3}$, where dev BLEU-4 stays above $12.7$ (above every reconstruction's maximum of $10.9$). (b) Dev BLEU-4, REC BLEU-4, PURE BLEU-4, and (at baseline) train-pool readout BLEU-4. The reversal and competence collapse together at $\sigma \geq 10^{-2}$; $\sigma =10^{-1}$ produces an all-empty-decoding model and is omitted. The reversal is locally robust at competence parity and tracks competence, not identity.\relax }{figure.caption.45}{}}
